/// \defgroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories Create modules, client modules and their factories
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Six create modules, three client update modules and six client
///        behavior modules (most of them sound modules), with the factory
///        functions that build them and their INI data.
///
/// Every class in this group is a module that an \c Object INI block lists
/// by name. Three kinds are covered, told apart by the module type and
/// interface mask they are registered with (the registrations are listed in
/// `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`):
/// - **Create modules** (ExperienceLevelCreate, GrantUpgradeCreate,
///   InheritUpgradeCreate, LockWeaponCreate, SpawnedModelConditionCreate,
///   SupplyCenterCreate) are logic (behavior) modules whose mask is the bit
///   Zero Hour uses for its create interface. In Zero Hour a create module
///   acts when its object is created or when its construction finishes.
///   The build-complete step is where the BFME 2 bodies described below do
///   their work; InheritUpgradeCreate also has a second create-interface
///   entry, described on its page, that runs the same work without the
///   build-complete check.
/// - **Client update modules** (BeaconClientUpdate, RadarMarkerClientUpdate
///   and, despite its name, EvaAnnounceClientCreate) use the module type and
///   interface bit Zero Hour uses for client updates: they belong to the
///   drawable and run on the client side.
/// - **Client behavior modules** (AnimationSoundClientBehavior,
///   ModelConditionAudioLoopClientBehavior, the three sound selectors and
///   TerrainResourceClientBehavior) use BFME 2's fourth module type, which
///   Zero Hour does not have, with an interface bit of their own
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_zh).
///
/// \par Two factories per module name
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance factory
/// and a data factory. Both are reached only through those registered
/// pointers, never called directly.
/// - The **data factory** runs while INI loads:
///   ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it with the INI reader for
///   each module line. It creates the module data with its constructor
///   defaults and, when the reader is not null, parses the module's block
///   into it with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse.
/// - The **instance factory** runs when an object or drawable is built:
///   ModuleFactory::newModule calls it with the new thing and the module
///   data parsed earlier, and it creates the module.
///
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories describes this flow step
/// by step for the special-power modules, and
/// \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories for the upgrade modules; it
/// applies here unchanged.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory allocates the module with the global
///   \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size, runs the
///   class's constructor with the thing and the module data, and returns
///   the module as the Module base the factory stores. An exception frame
///   around the constructor frees the block if the constructor throws, as a
///   C++ new-expression requires. When the allocation returns null the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned.
/// - Every data factory documented here has the same frame: allocate,
///   construct, then parse. Parsing happens after construction and outside
///   the exception frame, so an INI error thrown while parsing does not free
///   the new data.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Modules without data of their own
/// The registrations of SupplyCenterCreate and TerrainResourceClientBehavior
/// do not have a data factory of their own. They point at one generic data
/// factory that other modules without fields also use, including
/// BridgeTowerBehavior, PreorderCreate, SupplyWarehouseCreate,
/// SpecialPowerCreate, InactiveBody, AnimatedParticleSysBoneClientUpdate and
/// SwayClientUpdate in the module registration list. It builds a bare module
/// data object and passes no field table of its own to the INI reader.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template. It
///   is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from the
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - A create module belongs to its object. A client update or client
///   behavior module belongs to the object's drawable; the \c Drawable
///   constructor instantiates the client-behavior list
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_zh). Zero Hour: an object or drawable deletes its
///   modules when it is destroyed, and the module factory's destructor
///   deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Origins
/// Zero Hour has BeaconClientUpdate, GrantUpgradeCreate, LockWeaponCreate
/// and SupplyCenterCreate. Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has sources for
/// ExperienceLevelCreate, AnimationSoundClientBehavior and the three sound
/// selectors as well, so those classes already exist in BFME 1. This page
/// makes no claim about where the other classes first appear.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their module tags and
///   the fields listed on each class page. The field names on the class
///   pages are read from the retail field tables, including their retail
///   spelling.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names, the module type each one is registered
///   as (a client behavior cannot be listed where a logic module is
///   expected) and what each class does with its fields. A new kind of
///   module needs a new class and a new ModuleFactory::init registration
///   (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - Multiplayer: create modules run in the game logic, so every machine
///   must load identical values for them (\ref page_modding_rules). The
///   client modules work on the drawable; RandomSoundSelectorClientBehavior,
///   for example, draws its rolls from the client random stream, not the
///   logic one (\ref page_core_frameworks_random).
/// - Saves: a module with state of its own transfers it in its \c xfer
///   function (for example RandomSoundSelectorClientBehavior::xfer, which
///   saves the random roll and its frame; \ref grp_w3_module_xfer describes
///   the shared chain). A create module's state is saved by its object as
///   a block keyed by its module tag, and on load a block whose tag the
///   object no longer has is skipped (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle),
///   so renaming a create module's tag in a mod orphans that state in old
///   saves. Client modules belong to the drawable instead. Zero Hour saves a
///   drawable's modules the same way, keyed by tag, in Drawable::xfer; which
///   of the BFME 2 drawable's module lists are saved like this is not
///   established here.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// \c DelayBeforeAnnouncementMS (EvaAnnounceClientCreate) and
/// \c RadarPulseFrequency and \c RadarPulseDuration (BeaconClientUpdate)
/// are parsed by INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt. It multiplies the written
/// milliseconds by a frames-per-millisecond scale held in a global and
/// rounds up, so the module data stores whole logic frames, fixed when the
/// INI is read (\ref page_frame_loop_modding).
/// - BFME 2 already runs client and logic frames at different rates
///   (nominally 30 and 5 a second; \ref page_frame_loop). These two
///   modules are client update modules, which Drawable::updateDrawable
///   calls; GameClient::update calls Drawable::updateDrawable for each
///   drawable on a client frame when time is not frozen.
///   They still measure the Eva delay and the pulse interval in logic
///   frames, against the game logic's frame counter, so those intervals
///   follow logic time, not the number of client frames.
/// - BeaconClientUpdate::clientUpdate turns the stored pulse duration back
///   into seconds for its radar event by multiplying it by the engine's
///   seconds-per-logic-frame value. In BFME 2 that value is a writable
///   global, not a constant (Zero Hour: the compile-time
///   \c SECONDS_PER_LOGICFRAME_REAL). BFME 2's frame-rate setup derives it
///   from the logic rate it is given and, separately, recomputes the
///   parse-time duration scale. The round trip from milliseconds to frames
///   and back is therefore consistent only while both globals describe the
///   same logic rate, and durations already converted to frames at INI load
///   are not rescaled if the rate changes afterwards.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_gameclient_drawables, \ref sub_audio, \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class EvaAnnounceClientCreate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Client update module registered as "EvaAnnounceClientCreate":
///        once, after a delay, has the Eva announcer report an event about
///        a new object, chosen by how the local player relates to it.
///
/// Despite the "Create" in its name it is registered as a client update
/// module. A hook, whose identity is not established here, schedules the
/// announcement once: at least two logic frames after the current frame,
/// or later if \c DelayBeforeAnnouncementMS asks for more, and never before
/// logic frame 7. EvaAnnounceClientCreate::clientUpdate then waits for that
/// frame and announces once:
/// - With \c OnlyIfVisible set, it gives up for good when the drawable
///   fails its visibility checks (a drawable flag and a drawable test whose
///   exact meanings are not established here).
/// - It needs the drawable's object. With \c CountAsFirstSightedAnnoucement
///   set it also sets a flag on the drawable, which the field name suggests
///   counts as the first-sighted announcement.
/// - The event is \c AnnouncementEventOwner when the object's controlling
///   player is the local player (the player list entry the reconstruction
///   reads as the local player), \c AnnouncementEventAlly when the local
///   player is allied to the object and
///   \c AnnouncementEventEnemy when it is an enemy or neutral; any other
///   relationship announces nothing.
/// - It calls Eva::reportEvaEvent with the event and the drawable's
///   position. The call's other position argument carries the same
///   position only when \c UseObjectsPosition is set and is null
///   otherwise.
///
/// The module saves its own state through EvaAnnounceClientCreate::xfer.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields (EvaAnnounceClientCreateModuleData, from the retail table):
/// \c AnnouncementEventEnemy, \c AnnouncementEventAlly and
/// \c AnnouncementEventOwner (Eva events), \c DelayBeforeAnnouncementMS (a
/// duration, see the group page), and the booleans \c OnlyIfVisible,
/// \c CountAsFirstSightedAnnoucement (retail spelling, with "Annoucement"),
/// \c UseObjectsPosition and \c CreateFakeRadarEvent. What
/// \c CreateFakeRadarEvent does is not established here, nor is the parser
/// that turns the event names into Eva events (\ref sub_audio covers the
/// announcer).
///
/// \see EvaAnnounceClientCreate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      EvaAnnounceClientCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class EvaAnnounceClientCreateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for EvaAnnounceClientCreate: the three announcement
///        events, the delay and four switches.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// EvaAnnounceClientCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.

/// \fn Module *EvaAnnounceClientCreate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "EvaAnnounceClientCreate":
///        creates the module for one drawable.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The thing being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's EvaAnnounceClientCreateModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see EvaAnnounceClientCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *EvaAnnounceClientCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "EvaAnnounceClientCreate": creates
///        the module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see EvaAnnounceClientCreate::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class ExperienceLevelCreate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Create module registered as "ExperienceLevelCreate": when its
///        object's construction completes, raises the object to a set
///        experience level.
///
/// ExperienceLevelCreate::onBuildComplete adds, through the object's
/// experience tracker, the difference between \c LevelToGrant and the
/// object's current level. With \c MPOnly set it does this only when a
/// game-logic check passes; the reconstruction's notes describe that check
/// as a multiplayer-or-skirmish test, which the field name agrees with, but
/// it is not confirmed.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields (ExperienceLevelCreateModuleData, from the retail table):
/// \c LevelToGrant (an integer level) and \c MPOnly (a boolean).
///
/// \see ExperienceLevelCreate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      ExperienceLevelCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class ExperienceLevelCreateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for ExperienceLevelCreate: the level to grant and the
///        multiplayer-only switch.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// ExperienceLevelCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by
/// every object of the template.

/// \fn Module *ExperienceLevelCreate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "ExperienceLevelCreate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's ExperienceLevelCreateModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see ExperienceLevelCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *ExperienceLevelCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "ExperienceLevelCreate": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The constructor's defaults are
/// a level of -1 and \c MPOnly off.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see ExperienceLevelCreate::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class GrantUpgradeCreate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Create module registered as "GrantUpgradeCreate": grants the
///        upgrade named in its INI data (Zero Hour: to the object or its
///        player, on creation or build completion).
///
/// Zero Hour: on creation, an object that is not under construction gets
/// the upgrade named by \c UpgradeToGrant, provided \c ExemptStatus
/// includes the under-construction status; when construction completes,
/// it gets the upgrade, once. A player upgrade goes to the controlling
/// player, an object upgrade to the object; an unknown upgrade name is
/// skipped (debug builds assert). The BFME 2 module's create and build-complete steps are
/// not described here, so whether BFME 2 follows this exactly is not
/// established.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields (GrantUpgradeCreateModuleData, from the retail table):
/// \c UpgradeToGrant (an upgrade name, stored as text as in Zero Hour),
/// \c ExemptStatus (Zero Hour: object status flags) and
/// \c GiveOnBuildComplete (a boolean that Zero Hour does not have; the
/// name suggests it selects the build-complete case).
///
/// \see GrantUpgradeCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class GrantUpgradeCreateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for GrantUpgradeCreate: the upgrade name, the exempt
///        status and the build-complete switch.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// GrantUpgradeCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn ModuleData *GrantUpgradeCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "GrantUpgradeCreate": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The matching instance factory,
/// GrantUpgradeCreate::friend_newModuleInstance, has the shared shape
/// described on the group page.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see GrantUpgradeCreate


/// \class InheritUpgradeCreate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Create module registered as "InheritUpgradeCreate": copies a set
///        of upgrades to its object from a nearby object of the same player
///        that already has them, at least when construction completes.
///
/// InheritUpgradeCreate::onBuildComplete runs the work once, as Zero Hour's
/// create modules do (it checks and clears the module's build-complete
/// flag). A second create-interface entry, whose trigger is not established,
/// runs the same work directly, without the once-only check. Read
/// from the retail body, the work it then does is: query the partition
/// manager for objects within \c Radius of the object that pass
/// \c ObjectFilter; for each one controlled by the same player whose
/// upgrades include every upgrade named by \c Upgrade, give the object
/// each of those upgrades. The reconstruction labels the member roles of
/// that body as inferred.
///
/// InheritUpgradeCreate::xfer transfers the create-module base and a
/// version number of its own.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields (InheritUpgradeCreateModuleData, from the retail table):
/// \c Radius (a real distance), \c Upgrade (upgrade names, held as an
/// upgrade mask) and \c ObjectFilter (an object filter).
///
/// \see InheritUpgradeCreate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      InheritUpgradeCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class InheritUpgradeCreateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for InheritUpgradeCreate: search radius, upgrade set
///        and object filter.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// InheritUpgradeCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn Module *InheritUpgradeCreate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "InheritUpgradeCreate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's InheritUpgradeCreateModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see InheritUpgradeCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *InheritUpgradeCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "InheritUpgradeCreate": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The constructor sets the radius
/// to zero and initialises the upgrade set and the object filter.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see InheritUpgradeCreate::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class LockWeaponCreate
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Create module registered as "LockWeaponCreate": locks one of its
///        object's weapon slots.
///
/// Zero Hour: when construction completes, the module permanently locks
/// the weapon slot named by \c SlotToLock. The BFME 2 module's
/// build-complete step is not described here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Field (LockWeaponCreateModuleData, from the retail table):
/// \c SlotToLock, a name from a lookup list (Zero Hour: the weapon slot
/// names).
///
/// \see LockWeaponCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class LockWeaponCreateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_create_client_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for LockWeaponCreate: the weapon slot to lock.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// LockWeaponCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn ModuleData *LockWeaponCreateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "LockWeaponCreate": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The matching instance factory,
/// LockWeaponCreate::friend_newModuleInstance, has the shared shape
/// described on the group page.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Retail keeps a single copy of this data class's constructor and uses it
/// for other module data classes of the same size and shape as well: the
/// data factories registered for SiegeDockingBehavior and StancesBehavior
/// also build their data with it and then parse their own field tables. A
/// breakpoint on that constructor therefore also stops for those modules.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see LockWeaponCreate


